description:
`Report::generate()` fixes the overall shape of every report: header, then body, then footer. The header and footer have defaults; the **body has none**, so `Report` is abstract and each kind of report must supply one. (This arrangement, a public non-virtual function calling private virtual steps, is the *template method* pattern.)

Write `SalesReport`, derived from `Report`:

- constructed with a number of units sold and a price in cents per unit;
- its `body()` returns `"Units: 12\nRevenue: 6000\n"` (for 12 units at 500);
- it also overrides `header()` to return `"== SALES ==\n"`; the footer is left alone.

starter.solution.cpp:
#include <string>

class Report
{
public:
    virtual ~Report() = default;
    std::string generate() const { return header() + body() + footer(); }

private:
    virtual std::string header() const { return "== REPORT ==\n"; }
    virtual std::string body() const = 0;
    virtual std::string footer() const { return "== END ==\n"; }
};

// class SalesReport : public Report

solution.solution.cpp:
#include <string>

class Report
{
public:
    virtual ~Report() = default;
    std::string generate() const { return header() + body() + footer(); }

private:
    virtual std::string header() const { return "== REPORT ==\n"; }
    virtual std::string body() const = 0;
    virtual std::string footer() const { return "== END ==\n"; }
};

class SalesReport : public Report
{
public:
    SalesReport(int units, int centsEach) : m_units{ units }, m_cents{ centsEach } {}

private:
    std::string header() const override { return "== SALES ==\n"; }
    std::string body() const override
    {
        return "Units: " + std::to_string(m_units) + "\nRevenue: " + std::to_string(m_units * m_cents) + "\n";
    }

    int m_units{};
    int m_cents{};
};
